Hello,

I am working with a zero-inflated negative binomial model in which I so far
loop through cases as shown in the stan language reference.
here is a snippet from the model block.
(I am leaving out the other parts of the model, which essentially follow
the example model for a hierarchical model with multivariate priors
from the stan documentation )

for (n in 1:N) {
    theta_n <- dot_product(sx0[n],alpha);
    eta_n <- dot_product(sx[n],beta[g[n]]);
    phi_n <- phi[g[n]];
    if (y[n] == 0)
      increment_log_prob(log_sum_exp(bernoulli_logit_log(1,theta_n),
                             bernoulli_logit_log(0,theta_n)
                             + neg_binomial_2_log_log(y[n],eta_n,phi_n)));
    else
      increment_log_prob(bernoulli_logit_log(0,theta_n)
                   + neg_binomial_2_log_log(y[n],eta_n,phi_n));
  }

There are some things I could do to vectorize, but I am
uncertain if this would lead to a noticeable speed improvement.
Maybe somebody with the relevant knowledge can comment on this?

Please note that I did not test this code, so there are nearly certainly bugs in it.

A)
split cases according to zero and nonzero outcomes (y)
and calculate theta and eta outside the without looping through participants,
something like:
real theta[N];
row_vector[2] eta;
row_vector[2] eta_zero;
row_vector[2] eta_nonzero;
row_vector[2] theta_zero;
row_vector[2] theta_nonzero;
row_vector[2] phis;

\\ sx0 is the design matrix, alpha are the regression weights
\\ I am using the same regression weights for all groups for
\\ the zero inflation part of the model
theta <- alpha * sx0;

\\ loop throug M groups with different regression weights for groups
\\ group_start contains indices indicating in which row a group starts
\\ group_size contains integers indicating group sizes
for (k in 1:M) {
  eta <- append_row(eta,beta[k]*block(sx,group_start[k],1,group_size[k],K));
}

\\ now split eta, phi, and theta according to whether the criterion y is zero
for (k in 1:M) {
 eta_zero <- append_row(theta_zero,segment(eta,group_start_zero[k],group_size_zero[k]);
 theta_zero <- append_row(theta_zero,segment(eta,group_start_zero[k],group_size_zero[k]);
 eta_nonzero <- append_row(theta_nonzero,segment(eta,group_start_nonzero[k],group_size_nonzero[k]);
 theta_nonzero <- append_row(theta_nonzero,segment(eta,group_start_nonzero[k],group_size_nonzero[k]);
 phi_nonzero <- append_row(phi_nonzero, rep_row_vector(phi[k],group_size_nonzero[k]);
 phi_zero <- append_row(phi_zero, rep_row_vector(phi[k],group_size_zero[k])
}

I could not figure out quickly how to initialize an empty vector,
and I don't think that one broadcast into segments of a vector.
Therefore I use the ugly trick to start with a small vector that I then expand it ...

B)
Loop through zero y (because of the need to use log_sum_exp),
but use vectorization for nonzero y (I know this doesn't improve
the speed of increment_log_prob, but hope it makes
neg_binomial_2_log_log faster ...).

for (n in 3:(N_zero+2)) {
  increment_log_prob(log_sum_exp(bernoulli_logit_log(1,theta_zero[n]),
                                                    bernoulli_logit_log(0,theta_zero[n])
                                                    + neg_binomial_2_log_log(0,eta_zero[n],phi_zero[n])));
}
increment_log_prob(bernoulli_logit_log(0,tail(theta_nonzero,N_nonzero))
                   + neg_binomial_2_log_log(y_nonzero,tail(eta_nonzero,N_nonzero),tail(phi_nonzero,N_nonzero)));

What do you think?

Guido

