> On Oct 20, 2015, at 6:56 PM, Jiang Du <jiang...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> I guess there are a lot of things going on within the warming up iterations. If I set warmup=3, iteration=10, the whole process takes about 20 seconds. However, if I set warmup=30, iteration=130, the total time is not 20*10 seconds, and actually, the first 13 warmups took several minutes, and after a long time, the second 13 warmups is still running now.

There's a description in the part of the manual about algorithms.  We
recommend running at least 100 warmup iterations.  Usually the first
few don't do much because the scale's off.  Then they hit increments where
step size is fit, and the result is often a perceived hang on Stan's part
because it moves to doing way more steps per iteration to get stability.

Perhaps not surprisingly, it's very hard for us to read all these models
and figure out what they're going to do without spending lots of time
diving into the details.

- Bob

