Yes, sometimes the stable parameters are slower than the
ones that don't work.  Failing can be fast.  Or it can be
slow --- depends on whether the step size vanishes to zero
(slow failure) or you get arithmetic errors (fast failure).

> Due to scaling and shifting, I could remove the constraints for these parameters. Maybe this leads to the slowing but better exploration?

You should have the constraints on parameters defined so
that any values meeting the constraint have nonzero density.

- Bob

From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Thu Oct 22 17:49:52 2015
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2015 17:49:52 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] Reducing memory usage in a matrix factorization model
Message-ID: <Qv1ZumNtm6M.Q7wMzZhqBgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <Qv1ZumNtm6M.LTC8KKgmBgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <Qv1ZumNtm6M.LTC8KKgmBgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/Qv1ZumNtm6M/m/Q7wMzZhqBgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Yes, we have an arXiv paper now on the autodiff.  Roughly, it's
about 40 bytes per expression evaluated in the log density.

What will help is if you vectorize these:

>    for (v in 1:n_words) {
>        col(inv_gw, v) ~ inv_gamma(a_w, b_w);
>        col(w, v) ~ normal(zero_vec, col(inv_gw, v));
>    }

Reduce this to:

  to_vector(inv_gw) ~ inv_gamma(a_w, b_w);
  to_vector(w) ~ normal(0, to_vector(inv_gw));

Note that the normal broadcasts, so you don't need a zero vector,
just the zero argument.

And this:

>    for (n in 1:n_obs) {
>        logx[n] ~ normal(dot_product(col(w, rows_[n]), col(w, cols_[n]))
>                         + b[rows_[n]] + b[cols_[n]],
>                         sigma);
>    }

  {
    real mu[n];

    for (n in 1:n_obs)

      mu[n] <- dot_product(w[rows_[n]], w[cols_[n]]) + b[rows_[n]] + b[cols_[n]];

    logx ~ normal(mu, sigma);
  }

This is also moving w to a data type that's an array of vectors.
The reason to do this is both speed and size.  It only has to do log(sigma)
once, and it cuts down the number of expressions from n to 1.  And using
an array of vectors removes the need to copy out rows or columns.

- Bob

