> > Thanks a lot for your suggestion. In my model block of codes, I specify that
> >
> >     for (m in 1:M-1)
> >         gamma ~ dirichlet(alpha);
> >
> > So here I want gamma to follow a dirichlet distribution so that all phi's are constrained between 0 and 1 and of course, phi[1] and phi[4] are fixed as 0 and one.  Yes, you are right that I need to put constraints on both gamma and alpha (?). I will revise my codes, and update what I got.
> >
> > I have to admit that I didn't spend a lot of time going over the manual. I just quickly went over the regression section on ordered regression and multi-category logit models. But that's a mistake that I should not have made :(
> >
> > I haven't quite understood/know how to implement your suggestion here. But I will try and see what happens. Thank you very much.
> >
> > On Tuesday, October 27, 2015 at 2:02:56 PM UTC-4, Ben Goodrich wrote:
> > On Tuesday, October 27, 2015 at 1:41:11 PM UTC-4, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> > Yeah, making gamma a simplex is a much better idea, though
> > that won't give you the gamma0 = 0 thing.
> >
> > Right. Just need to make the simplex vector one element shorter and take gamma0 = 0 to be fixed.
