Thanks, Bob.

Yes, that is the error message. The only thing I included is the label for the "1" column is "count."

I'm working on setting a prior on the differences. Say, I choose a truncated normal. In this instance,

~normal(0,10)T[0,]

will not with the tail(tau, C-2)-head(tau, C-2) vector. If I define a local variable in the model block, say,

vector<lower=0>[C-2] dif_g1;
dif_g1<-tail(tau, C-2)-head(tau, C-2);

I get the following error, because I don't think constraints can be applied to local variables (at least from my read of the manual, though perhaps I am mistaken):

  require unconstrained. found range constraint.

Is there a way to ensure the difference prior distribution is greater than 0?

I also tried the following code in the model block (the rest of the code from before is the same), with uniform priors on the differences, though this produces the original warning message (e.g., "Exception thrown at line 69: ordered_logistic: Cut points parameter is -154.509, but must be greater" ). Is it clear that I am missing something? Thanks again. I really appreciate it.

model{
  for(k in 1:K-1){

    tau_g1[k,1]~normal(0,10); // k is the item. Since the first item is the anchor, loop through the remaining K-1 items.
    tau_g2[k,1]~normal(0,10);
    tau_g3[k,1]~normal(0,10);
    tail(tau_g1[k], C-2)-head(tau_g1[k], C-2)~uniform(0,1000); //C is the number of categories, the tau_g vector is length C-1, so set a prior on the differences for each item
    tail(tau_g2[k], C-2)-head(tau_g2[k], C-2)~uniform(0,1000);
    tail(tau_g3[k], C-2)-head(tau_g3[k], C-2)~uniform(0,1000);
  }
  tau[1]~normal(0,10);
  tail(tau, C-2)-head(tau, C-2)~uniform(0,1000); //Same here, but tau is the threshold vector common across groups.

  m1~normal(0,0.001);
  m2~normal(0,10);
  m3~normal(0,10);

  theta_g1 ~ normal(m1,1); //Standard normal for identification.

  theta_g2 ~ normal(m2,1);
  theta_g3 ~ normal(m3,1);
  delta ~    cauchy(0,5);
  delta_g1 ~ cauchy(0,5);
  delta_g2 ~ cauchy(0,5);
  delta_g3 ~ cauchy(0,5);

  for (n in 1:N_G1){
    if(kk_G1[n]==1){
      y_G1[n] ~ ordered_logistic(delta*theta_g1[jj_G1[n]], tau);
    }
    else{

      y_G1[n] ~ ordered_logistic(delta_g1[kk_G1[n]-1]*theta_g1[jj_G1[n]], tau_g1[kk_G1[n]-1]); //-1 is included to fix the indexing, it ranges from 1-4, but should be 1-3, since 1 is the anchor

    }
  }

  for (n in 1:N_G2){
    if(kk_G2[n]==1){
      y_G2[n] ~ ordered_logistic(delta*theta_g2[jj_G2[n]], tau);
    }
    else{
      y_G2[n] ~ ordered_logistic(delta_g2[kk_G2[n]-1]*theta_g2[jj_G2[n]], tau_g2[kk_G2[n]-1]);
    }
  }

  for (n in 1:N_G3){
    if(kk_G3[n]==1){
      y_G3[n] ~ ordered_logistic(delta*theta_g3[jj_G3[n]], tau);
    }
    else{
      y_G3[n] ~ ordered_logistic(delta_g3[kk_G3[n]-1]*theta_g3[jj_G3[n]], tau_g3[kk_G3[n]-1]);
    }
  }
}

