Hi Bob,

OK, then I thing I get what these divergences are about. And yes there
are warnings printed.
I think it happens mostly when zeros are drawn for the sigmas of the
normal distributions on the group level. They are defined with lower=0,
but i guess this may still produce some zeros during the first few draws.

For 5000 samples I now got 11 divergences. Getting a few during the
warmup shouldn't do too much harm, do they?

I found a possible origin of strange sampling. I had reparametrized some
of the parameters in this non-central way:
value[i] <- mean + e[i] * sigma

While this works great for most parameters, one was producing absurdly
high (and autocorrelated) samples in the e[i] while the sigma was
approaching zero. I guess the reason was that that this parameter is not
normalized and lives on a much larger scale that the others for which it
works.

Thanks
Jan

On 10/29/2015 06:53 PM, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> It means 23 times you ran into arithmetic troubles due to
> underflow, overflow, divide by zero, subtracting two infinite
> values, etc.  You really want that number to be zero.
>
> You can try running with a higher target acceptance rate and
> perhaps smaller initial step size.  Youc ould start with
>
>    control = list(stepsize=0.01, adapt_delta=0.95)
>
> and keep lowering.  By reducing the step size (and targeting
> higher accept stat does that post warmup), the first-order
> approximation of curvature via stepping according to the gradient
