> No, no, no. We _are_ talking about phylogenetics. Again, you can't get away from the prior assumption of lack of independence among species. And that lack of independence can be represented a priori by the phylogeny. In fact, the phylogenetic information is the only objective way of incorporating that information. Whether that information contributes to the posterior is then an empirical question as the model learns.
